Below is a **complete, modular skeleton** of a distributed SQL‑query execution engine written in C#.  
It is split into three independent building blocks that you asked for:

| # | Component | Responsibility |
|---|-----------|-----------------|
| 1 | **NetworkExchangeOperator** | Buffers rows, partitions them, and ships each partition to the appropriate remote node (or keeps it locally). |
| 2 | **HashAggregationOperator** | Consumes rows, groups them by a key, maintains an in‑memory hash table, and asks the spill manager when the memory budget is exceeded. |
| 3 | **DiskSpillManager** | Persists overflow hash‑table buckets to temporary files, reads them back on demand, and cleans them up when no longer needed. |

The code is deliberately **plug‑and‑play** – each piece implements a small set of interfaces, so you can replace the network stack, the serialization format, or the spill strategy without touching the other parts.

---

## 1️⃣ Core Types & Contracts

```csharp
// ---------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. Row & Schema
// ---------------------------------------------------------------
public sealed class Row
{
    // Simple column‑value container – you can replace it with a
    // more sophisticated columnar representation later.
    public IReadOnlyList<object?> Values { get; }

    public Row(params object?[] values) => Values = values;
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. Operator contracts – everything in the engine is an IOperator.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
public interface IOperator : IAsyncDisposable
{
    // Pull based – the consumer asks for the next batch.
    ValueTask<IReadOnlyList<Row>> GetNextBatchAsync(CancellationToken ct = default);
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. Partitioning contract – used by the exchange operator.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
public interface IPartitioner
{
    // Returns a partition id (0 … PartitionCount‑1) for a given row.
    int GetPartition(Row row);
    int PartitionCount { get; }
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// 4. Spill manager contract – used by the aggregation operator.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
public interface ISpillManager : IAsyncDisposable
{
    // Called when a bucket must be persisted.
    ValueTask SpillAsync(int bucketId, IDictionary<object?, AggregationState> bucket, CancellationToken ct = default);

    // Called when a bucket is needed again.
    ValueTask<IDictionary<object?, AggregationState>> LoadAsync(int bucketId, CancellationToken ct = default);
}
```

> **Why async?**  
> Distributed execution is I/O‑bound (network + disk). All public APIs are async to avoid thread‑pool starvation and to make back‑pressure easy to implement.

---

## 2️⃣ NetworkExchangeOperator – Buffering & Routing

```csharp
using System.Net.Sockets;
using System.Buffers.Binary;
using System.Collections.Concurrent;
using System.Text.Json;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------
// 2.1  Exchange configuration
// ---------------------------------------------------------------
public sealed class ExchangeConfig
{
    public IPartitioner Partitioner { get; init; } = default!;
    public IReadOnlyDictionary<int, EndPoint> RemoteEndpoints { get; init; } = default!; // partition → remote node
    public int LocalPartitionId { get; init; } = -1; // -1 => this node does NOT own any partition
    public int BatchSize { get; init; } = 4096; // rows per network packet
    public TimeSpan FlushInterval { get; init; } = TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(100);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------
// 2.2  NetworkExchangeOperator
// ---------------------------------------------------------------
public sealed class NetworkExchangeOperator : IOperator
{
    private readonly ExchangeConfig _cfg;
    private readonly IOperator _upstream; // e.g. a scan or a previous operator
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private readonly ConcurrentDictionary<int, PartitionBuffer> _buffers = new();
    private readonly Task _flusherTask;

    public NetworkExchangeOperator(ExchangeConfig cfg, IOperator upstream)
    {
        _cfg = cfg;
        _upstream = upstream;

        // one buffer per partition (including the local one)
        for (int p = 0; p < cfg.Partitioner.PartitionCount; p++)
            _buffers[p] = new PartitionBuffer(cfg.BatchSize);

        // start the background flusher that ships data periodically
        _flusherTask = Task.Run(FlusherLoopAsync);
    }

    // ----------------------------------------------------------------
    // Pull‑based implementation – we simply forward batches from upstream,
    // partition them and store them in the buffers.
    // ----------------------------------------------------------------
    public async ValueTask<IReadOnlyList<Row>> GetNextBatchAsync(CancellationToken ct = default)
    {
        var batch = await _upstream.GetNextBatchAsync(ct).ConfigureAwait(false);
        foreach (var row in batch)
        {
            int part = _cfg.Partitioner.GetPartition(row);
            _buffers[part].Add(row);
        }

        // The exchange itself does not produce rows for the next operator.
        // It only forwards rows that belong to the *local* partition.
        if (_cfg.LocalPartitionId >= 0)
            return _buffers[_cfg.LocalPartitionId].DrainAllAndReset();
        else
            return Array.Empty<Row>();
    }

    // ----------------------------------------------------------------
    // Background flusher – ships full buffers or flushes on timeout.
    // ----------------------------------------------------------------
    private async Task FlusherLoopAsync()
    {
        var timer = new PeriodicTimer(_cfg.FlushInterval);
        try
        {
            while (await timer.WaitForNextTickAsync(_cts.Token).ConfigureAwait(false))
            {
                foreach (var kvp in _buffers)
                {
                    int partId = kvp.Key;
                    var buf = kvp.Value;

                    if (buf.IsEmpty) continue;
                    if (partId == _cfg.LocalPartitionId) continue; // already consumed locally

                    // Ship the buffer to its remote node
                    await SendPartitionAsync(partId, buf.DrainAllAndReset(), _cts.Token).ConfigureAwait(false);
                }
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) { /* normal shutdown */ }
    }

    // ----------------------------------------------------------------
    // Simple TCP sender – you can replace this with gRPC, ZeroMQ, etc.
    // ----------------------------------------------------------------
    private async Task SendPartitionAsync(int partitionId, IReadOnlyList<Row> rows,
                                          CancellationToken ct)
    {
        if (!_cfg.RemoteEndpoints.TryGetValue(partitionId, out var ep)) return;

        using var client = new TcpClient();
        await client.ConnectAsync(ep, ct).ConfigureAwait(false);
        using var net = client.GetStream();

        // 1️⃣ write a small header (partition id + row count)
        var header = new byte[8];
        BinaryPrimitives.WriteInt32LittleEndian(header, partitionId);
        BinaryPrimitives.WriteInt32LittleEndian(header.AsSpan(4), rows.Count);
        await net.WriteAsync(header, ct).ConfigureAwait(false);

        // 2️⃣ serialize rows (JSON for demo – replace with binary codec)
        foreach (var row in rows)
        {
            var json = JsonSerializer.Serialize(row.Values);
            var len = BitConverter.GetBytes(json.Length);
            await net.WriteAsync(len, ct).ConfigureAwait(false);
            var data = System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(json);
            await net.WriteAsync(data, ct).ConfigureAwait(false);
        }

        await net.FlushAsync(ct).ConfigureAwait(false);
    }

    // ----------------------------------------------------------------
    // Clean‑up
    // ----------------------------------------------------------------
    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();
        await _flusherTask.ConfigureAwait(false);
        await _upstream.DisposeAsync().ConfigureAwait(false);
        _cts.Dispose();
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------
    // Helper: per‑partition in‑memory buffer
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------
    private sealed class PartitionBuffer
    {
        private readonly List<Row> _rows = new(_batchSize);
        private readonly int _batchSize;
        public bool IsEmpty => _rows.Count == 0;

        public PartitionBuffer(int batchSize) => _batchSize = batchSize;

        public void Add(Row r) => _rows.Add(r);
        public IReadOnlyList<Row> DrainAllAndReset()
        {
            var snapshot = _rows.ToArray(); // copy – caller may keep reference
            _rows.Clear();
            return snapshot;
        }
    }
}
```

### How it works

1. **Upstream** pushes rows to the exchange via `GetNextBatchAsync`.  
2. Each row is handed to the **partitioner** (`IPartitioner`) which returns a numeric partition id.  
3. The row is stored in a **per‑partition buffer** (`PartitionBuffer`).  
4. A **background flusher** (timer‑driven) ships non‑local buffers to the remote node that owns the partition.  
5. For the **local partition** the exchange simply returns the drained rows, so the next operator receives them as if they came from a normal source.

> **Back‑pressure** – If a remote node is slow, the TCP `WriteAsync` will await, causing the flusher to stall. The upstream will eventually see the buffers fill up and can throttle its own production (you can add a `SemaphoreSlim` per buffer to enforce a max‑size).

